{"subject":"URGENT: Legal Review Required - Response to House Oversight Committee (Potential False Statement / 18 U.S.C. 1001)","to":["Office of the Solicitor"],"body":"Following up on the request for legal review sent earlier today. Congressional Affairs has directed the response drafting team to proceed with an aggregate response attributing all seven reassignments to reorganization and workload rebalancing, omitting the July 22 email from the Rawlins Field Manager and omitting any mention that the two Rawlins reassignments were prompted by employee communications with Congress.

Given that the House Oversight Committee specifically asked "whether any [reassignments, details, or adverse actions] were prompted by employees' communications with Congress," submitting a signed response from the Director that denies or conceals the basis documented in the July 22 email presents direct exposure under 18 U.S.C. § 1001 (false statements to Congress) and 5 U.S.C. § 7211.

Because Congressional Affairs' instruction directs the omission of material facts directly responsive to Congress's inquiry, we require formal concurrence or guidance from the Office of the Solicitor before drafting or transmitting a response to the Director for signature."}

